🟢 C++ Module 08 - Templated Containers, Iterators, and Algorithms

"A deep dive into the heart of the C++ Standard Template Library (STL)."

📖 About the Project

This project from the 42 curriculum aims to introduce the advanced C++ ecosystem, encouraging the use of Containers, Iterators, and Algorithms from the standard library (STL) instead of manually implementing methods using C. Additionally, all developed classes follow the strict standards of the Orthodox Canonical Form.

🚀 Challenges and Exercises

🔍 Ex00: Easy Find

Objective: Introduction to function templates and algorithms.

Implemented a generic function template called easyfind, which takes a generic type T (representing an integer container) and an integer to search for.

Uses algorithms from the <algorithm> library to find the first occurrence of the value, throwing an appropriate exception if the element does not exist.

📏 Ex01: Span

Objective: Advanced encapsulation and large-scale data manipulation.

Created the Span class, capable of storing a maximum of N integers, as specified in its constructor.

Implemented the shortestSpan() and longestSpan() methods to calculate the shortest and longest distances between all stored numbers.

Highlight: Implemented a dedicated template method to populate the container with thousands of numbers at once using an iterator range (begin and end). Successfully tested with more than 10,000 numbers.

🧬 Ex02: Mutated Abomination (MutantStack)

Objective: Inheritance and exploration of container adapters.

The standard C++ std::stack container is not directly iterable by default.

Created the generic MutantStack class to overcome this limitation. By directly inheriting from the original stack, it accesses its protected components and provides full iterator support, transforming the stack into an iterable data structure while preserving its fundamental LIFO (Last In, First Out) behavior.

🛠️ Technologies and Guidelines

Code is written strictly in the C++98 standard.

Strict compilation using c++ with the -Wall -Wextra -Werror flags.

Complete prevention of memory leaks in dynamic memory allocations.

🏁 How to Run

The project is divided into separate directories for each exercise. To compile and test any of them, navigate to the desired directory and use the included Makefile:

# Navigate to the exercise directory (e.g., ex01)
cd ex01/

# Compile the project
make

# Run the generated executable
./Span
